[SIZE=2]Current release: v1.2.5 beta (build 125)  |  [URL='https://github.com/akilluminati47/RIPPS2/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md']Changelog[/URL]  |  [URL='https://github.com/akilluminati47/RIPPS2/releases']All releases[/URL][/SIZE][/CENTER]

Hey everyone. RIPPS2 is in beta, and the download link above always gets the newest build.

[SIZE=3]RIPPS2 is a PS2 front end in a single .elf (2,714 KB): a teardown and rebuild of [URL='https://github.com/NathanNeurotic/Open-PS2-Loader']RiptOPL[/URL], the Open PS2 Loader fork, with rickgaiser's [URL='https://github.com/rickgaiser/neutrino']Neutrino[/URL] inside as its second game core, and its own look, launchers, file browser and save tools. Glass towers stand behind your games and orbits behind your files, all drawn live by the PS2. The video is RIPPS2 running in PCSX2, from the web page it started as to what it does now; its only music is a PS2's own, the menu's and the red screen's, ripped from its BIOS by RIPPS2 itself.[/SIZE]

[SIZE=5][B]Launch Disc[/B][/SIZE]
[LIST]
[*][LEFT]Boots PS2, PS1 and DVD video discs, and names the game in the tray before you start it.[/LEFT]
[*][LEFT]Plays audio CDs right on the page, with the towers turned into a spectrum analyzer.[/LEFT]
[*][LEFT][B]RIPDISC[/B]: hold SELECT to keep the disc in the drive. A PS2 game becomes an ISO in your games list, a PS1 game a BIN and CUE for Ember, an audio CD a folder of WAVs and a DVD video its VIDEO_TS files, with the time it will take said up front.[/LEFT]
[/LIST]

[SIZE=5][B]Storage[/B][/SIZE]
[LIST]
[*][LEFT][B]Fast Boot[/B] (Settings > Game Sources > Cache): RIPPS2 boots from its own drive alone, every list from its cache, and the other drives start when you need them. A drive set to Manual still shows its games on its page, and X on one starts the drive and the game.[/LEFT]
[*][LEFT][B]ART Pack[/B] (Settings > Game Sources > Cache): every started drive's ART folder in one art.tar on the drive you pick, the fastest first. Covers load from that one file through one index, never one lookup per game, so the art keeps up and the music stays smooth. New art goes in a .tar of its own and is merged into the pack, the file names kept. Your ART folders are not touched.[/LEFT]
[*][LEFT]Every game on every drive in one list, [B]All Games[/B], whole from the first frame, before the drives even answer; or drive by drive: USB, the internal HDD (exFAT or APA, shown once), iLink, MX4SIO, MMCE and the network.[/LEFT]
[*][LEFT]Art and an info page with the game's badges, five-star rating, genre, developer, release and size; the screenshots crossfade, and the right stick tips the page like a case in your hand.[/LEFT]
[*][LEFT]Per game: [B]Hide Game[/B] (back any time from Game Sources > Hidden Games), [B]Delete[/B] completely (the image, its settings, art and cheats; its saves only if you say so; on APA the partition table is checked first), and [B]Compress to ZSO[/B] (experimental: every piece checked before the ISO goes, and Back keeps both; an MMCE card needs [URL='https://sd2psxtd.github.io/download']sd2psXtd[/URL] 1.4.0 or newer), plus the loader core, cheats, VMCs, GSM, controller macros and achievements.[/LEFT]
[*][LEFT][B]The launch guard[/B]: pick a game while a drive is still being read and a panel says what RIPPS2 is finishing, then starts the game by itself. Nothing is ever written half way through a launch.[/LEFT]
[*][LEFT]R3 stars a game into Starred Games, L2 + R2 picks a random one. The in-game menu (L1 + L2 + R1 + R2 + SELECT + START): Resume, Exit Game, [B]RIPSHOT[/B] (the frame that was on screen, as your theme's screenshot art or full size), Achievements and Power Off.[/LEFT]
[*][LEFT]Cover art fetched by the PS2 itself in [B]HD[/B] (280 x 400) or Standard (140 x 200, as OPL's), 8-bit with dithering, a game that has one left alone. [B]RIPFLOW shows five covers on a 16:9 picture[/B] (720p, 1080i, or Widescreen on), three on 4:3. 720p and 1080i drawn at full detail.[/LEFT]
[/LIST]

[SIZE=5][B]Memory Files[/B][/SIZE]
[LIST]
[*][LEFT]A full file manager: copy, cut, paste and delete across memory cards, USB, HDD and MMCE, with a clean replace (it asks first, then erases, copies and checks the new file). The open folder is the first row, showing its entry count, marked count, size and the drive's free space; Cross or Square on it marks all or none.[/LEFT]
[*][LEFT]SEARCH FILES: type part of a name and every drive is searched; a result opens in its own folder.[/LEFT]
[*][LEFT][B]A CD player for your own tracks[/B]: mark .wav, .ogg or .mp3 files and open them together, and they play as one disc over the towers.[/LEFT]
[*][LEFT]OPL's VMC card images open like folders; PSU export and import; a text editor; pictures; XviD and DivX AVI [B]video full screen[/B], with [B]video player controls[/B]: Cross plays/pauses, Circle stops, START loops, L1/R1 jump 10 s, L2/R2 volume (shown as a number and bar, fades when you let go); ISO, ZSO and PS1 VCD games start right from the browser.[/LEFT]
[*][LEFT][B]A .tar is a blue folder[/B]: open it to see what it holds, PASTE pictures in, COPY members out, DELETE takes them out. Each change is written beside the archive and swapped in only once whole.[/LEFT]
[*][LEFT]3D save icons, textured, lit and animated, and RIP 3D ICON writes any icon out as OBJ, MTL and PNG for a PC.[/LEFT]
[*][LEFT]Square on a memory card opens MCKILLA, the card manager: back a card up to a VMC, write saves back, erase, format.[/LEFT]
[/LIST]

[SIZE=5][B]Themes[/B][/SIZE]
[LIST]
[*][LEFT]RIPGRID (a cover grid whose cases turn over to their back covers) and RIPFLOW (a cover carousel) are built in; Adapt and Adapt Rx come in the theme pack, with the three categories as glyphs in their side bar. Every theme can have the sidebar (Layout > Category Layout > Sidebar): Circle opens it, L1 / R1 stay the theme's own. On RIPFLOW, Up / Down tilt the centre case. Hold SELECT on a game list for the [B]theme list[/B]: pick one and it loads.[/LEFT]
[*][LEFT][B]Color Themes[/B] (RIPPS2, Ember, Blood, Ectoplasm, Amethyst, Bone, Tangerine, Flamingo, Turquoise, Moonlight, or Custom in your own color), and the [B]Color Sweep[/B]: five colors the theme fades through, never fast enough to flash, or a sweep of your own.[/LEFT]
[*][LEFT][B]Your launch[/B]: as a game starts, its disc comes in your way on any theme: Pop In, Spin or Zoom.[/LEFT]
[*][LEFT]A theme can place its art and text for each video mode, so 1080i gets its own layout and 480i keeps its own.[/LEFT]
[*][LEFT]Themes made for OPL and RiptOPL work as they were made: Storage alone, with Launch Disc and Memory Files in the START menu.[/LEFT]
[*][LEFT]Make your own with [URL='https://github.com/akilluminati47/RIPPS2-themes']RIPPS2-themes[/URL]: every key in one guide, a checker, no rebuild.[/LEFT]
[/LIST]

[SIZE=5][B]On board, and found[/B][/SIZE]
[LIST]
[*][LEFT]Neutrino 1.8.0 rides inside the .elf. Your own POPSTARTER, the POPS files, Ember and its BIOS are searched for on your drives and remembered (Settings > Game Sources > Cache > Look now). STATUS on the START menu opens on your drives, their format and free space, then the launchers it found.[/LEFT]
[*][LEFT]BIBLE: PS2BBL 1.2.0 set up for RIPPS2 on an 8 MB memory card or the internal HDD, so the console boots straight into it, from where RIPPS2 already is (it never copies itself). On an SD2PSX it sets BOOT LOCK in the card's own PS2BBL Extended. Already have a boot loader? BOOT LOCK sets your own PS2BBL, FreeMcBoot or OSDMenu to start RIPPS2, and a game's EXIT comes back through it.[/LEFT]
[*][LEFT]SpiritofRA: RetroAchievements through the xeRAbora client on your PC, with badges and YOUR ACCOUNT.[/LEFT]
[*][LEFT]Settings in RIPPS2's own memory card save (mc0:/RIPPS2, with its own icon), apart from any other OPL, or on any drive you pick (Settings > General > Save Settings To). Every settings page fits one screen.[/LEFT]
[*][LEFT]33 languages, Arabic and Persian right to left: every release carries [URL='https://github.com/akilluminati47/RIPPS2/releases/latest/download/RIPPS2-languages.zip']RIPPS2-languages.zip[/URL], kept up to date with each build. Copy its LNG folder to your drive and pick one in Settings > Interface > Language.[/LEFT]
[/LIST]

[SIZE=5][B]Sound from your own console[/B][/SIZE]
[LIST]
[*][LEFT]No one's sounds ship with RIPPS2. Audio Settings > BIOS Sounds rips your PS2's own browser sounds from its BIOS (under 1 MB), and BIOS Music renders its menu music (the clock's piece, 5:04, or the red screen's, or both) as a WAV, telling you the size first and letting you pick the folder.[/LEFT]
[*][LEFT]Any WAV, Ogg or MP3 in your AUDIO folder plays as music, on every theme, and you choose where a rip goes (beside RIPPS2 is read first at boot). The boot sound is the first thing read at boot, so it plays as the towers appear.[/LEFT]
[*][LEFT]On a PC, [URL='https://github.com/akilluminati47/RIPPS2/tree/main/tools/media-studio']RIPPS2 Media Studio[/URL] turns any sound, song or video into what RIPPS2 plays ([URL='https://github.com/akilluminati47/RIPPS2/releases/download/media-studio/RIPPS2-Media-Studio.zip']download it for Windows[/URL]: unzip, double-click).[/LEFT]
[/LIST]

[SIZE=5][B]How to run it[/B][/SIZE]
[URL='https://github.com/akilluminati47/RIPPS2/releases/latest/download/RIPPS2.elf']Download the latest RIPPS2.elf[/URL], copy it to a USB stick or a memory card, and start it from FreeMcBoot, PS2BBL, wLaunchELF or whatever you already use for homebrew. In PCSX2: System > Start File. For the themes, unzip [URL='https://github.com/akilluminati47/RIPPS2-themes/releases/latest/download/RIPPS2-themes.zip']the latest pack[/URL] into a THM folder on your drive and pick one in Settings > Interface > Theme.

